 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N \cdot M)$, where $N$ is the number of pixels and $M$ is the number of steps per ray.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ per pixel (excluding depth buffer and shadow maps).
-*   **Strengths:** Simple implementation; allows early-out optimization; works well for uniform or simple procedural fog [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** High computational cost for large volumes or small step sizes; prone to noise without temporal or spatial filtering; slice artifacts require jittering [S2, unofficial].
-*   **When to Use:** Real-time applications requiring simple aerial perspective or light shafts with limited budget [S2, unofficial].
-*   **When Not to Use:** Complex, non-uniform volumes requiring high accuracy; scenes with many dynamic light sources requiring shadow maps for every sample [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of samples $N$ along the ray.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Constant, requiring only per-pixel accumulators.
+*   **Strengths:** Simple to implement in a full-screen shader; allows for early-out optimization; simulates dynamic lighting interactions (soft shadows, light shafts) [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Slice artifacts require jittering; jittering can introduce noise; performance degrades with large step sizes or high resolution; assumes uniform fog unless additional data structures (textures/OpenVDB) are used [S2, unofficial].
+*   **When to use:** Real-time applications requiring atmospheric effects and dynamic lighting interaction where backward ray-marching is not feasible or where early-out optimization is beneficial [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Volumetric fog is a medium that emits or absorbs light, used for aerial perspective [S1]. The technique of ray-marching fog in games, such as in *Horizon Zero Dawn*, *Death Stranding*, and *Returnal*, treats fog as participating media traced via ray-marching [S2, unofficial]. The specific forward ray-marching implementation described here follows the approach of Toth and Umenhofer (Real-time Volumetric Lighting in Participating Media) but adapts it for forward marching to enable early-out optimizations [S2, unofficial].
+Ray-marched volumetric fog is a technique used in real-time graphics to simulate participating media. It is based on the volume rendering integral and approximates it using Riemann sums [S2, unofficial]. The specific implementation described follows the forward ray-marching approach, deviating from the backward ray-marching used in the Real-time Volumetric Lighting in Participating Media paper by Toth and Umenhofer [S2, unofficial]. This technique is used in games like Horizon Zero Dawn, Death Stranding, and Returnal [S2, unofficial].
 
